setupapi: fewer registry round trips when device lists are made and destroyed.

In b161 (Proton d2r10) one D2R thread (tid 764, unnamed, game code) made a list of the HID
device interfaces with SetupDiGetClassDevs and destroyed it again twice a second for the whole
run: exactly 70 key opens and 54 value reads a second, every second (FEX's registry notes).
That is 35 opens and 27 value reads per list of 3 devices, which is what Wine's SetupAPI code
does for 3 present interfaces: 2 opens for the interface class, 7 opens and 6 value reads per
device to add it, and 4 opens and 3 value reads per device to destroy it. xinput's HID thread
(wine_xinput_hid_update) does the same for its one device when no report came for 2 s: 13
opens and 9 value reads (2 + 7 + 4, 6 + 3). By the code, each list of 3 also costs 16 key
creates, 12 value writes and 48 closes: about 300 wineserver requests a second.

Two parts of that are repeat work whose answer can't change:
- Destroying a device asks its installers to free their private data (DIF_DESTROYPRIVATEDATA):
  CoDeviceInstallers (not found), the driver key and its CoInstallers32 (not found), the class
  key and its Installer32 (not found), for each device every time. An installer only has
  private data for a device it was called for in this set, so the call is now made only for a
  device that SetupDiCallClassInstaller ran for. An app that installs drivers calls the
  installers first and still gets it.
- Every device added looks up its class name (Control\Class, the class key, the Class value).
  While one SetupDiGetClassDevs call fills a list it made itself, devices of the same class
  share the first lookup. The name is kept only during that call, and only after a lookup
  that worked.

Per list of 3: 16 opens, 11 value reads, 3 creates and 16 closes fewer (46 of ~150
requests, ~90 a second for D2R's thread). FEX's registry line should show 190 opens and 160 value
reads per 5 s for tid 764 instead of 350 and 270. WINE_D2R_SETUPAPI_FAST=0 turns both off.

Tests: model test (/home/claude/pw/satest, the patch's own function text with mocked
registry lookups): 500 seeds x 200 lists with random classes, registry changes between lists
and lists given back as deviceset, 0 differences in the class names written, lookups and
destroy calls as expected; switch 0/1 read once; negative controls (cache kept after the call,
installer call not noted) caught with 808 and 500 differences.
